        heading: "The concise answer",
        body: "Shooting range ventilation design creates a one-directional laminar airflow system that pushes clean supply air from behind the firing line downrange toward the bullet trap, carrying lead dust, powder fumes, and combustion gases away from occupants. The design specifies supply and exhaust volumes, the velocity profile across the range, filtration of exhaust air, heating and cooling of the large outside-air volumes, and controls that prove the airflow pattern holds under all operating conditions.\n\nThe health stakes are what make this design non-negotiable. Lead exposure is cumulative and serious, and range staff breathe this air for eight hours a day. I treat range ventilation as life-safety engineering: the airflow pattern gets designed, then verified — and the owner gets a system with monitoring that proves it's working, not just equipment that was installed.",
