    title: "What Engineering Does a Nonprofit Community Center Require?",
    description: "Community centers combine gymnasiums, commercial kitchens, classrooms, and event halls — the MEP and structural design must serve every single use at once.",
    h1: "What Engineering Does a Community Center Require?",
    answer: "A nonprofit community center is one of the hardest buildings to engineer per square foot, because it's really five buildings sharing one roof: a gymnasium, a commercial kitchen, classrooms, an event hall, and offices — each with different structural, mechanical, and plumbing needs, all funded by a budget that has no room for waste. I've worked with nonprofits who got a donated design that treated the building like offices with a big room attached, and the result was a gym that echoed, a kitchen the health department wouldn't approve, and HVAC that couldn't handle a full event hall. The engineering has to honor every use the community was promised, on a budget that demands efficiency, not shortcuts.",
    directAnswer: "Community center engineering is the structural, mechanical, electrical, and plumbing design of a multi-use public facility. The package typically includes the gymnasium structure with long spans and acoustic treatment, HVAC zoned for each distinct space — gym, kitchen, classrooms, event hall — commercial kitchen MEP with health department compliance, plumbing for restrooms, locker rooms, and kitchen demands, electrical and lighting for assembly and sports uses, and accessibility and life-safety design for public occupancy.",
